"Shows movement between geographic origins and destinations using spatially anchored paths and explicit direction or volume encodings.\n\nUse when: Explain travel, migration, trade or logistics connections between places. Compare where flows begin and end while retaining geographic context.\n\nAvoid when: The question concerns nonspatial transfers or the density of connections would make one view unreadable.\n\nVisual grammar: Origin and destination locations anchor flow lines. Arrowheads or ordered endpoints encode direction. Width encodes magnitude only under a stated scale; drawn curves need not be actual routes.\n\nBuild: Define the movement unit, time window and geographic endpoints. Choose a consistent direction and magnitude convention. Split by origin, destination or layer when real flows would cross densely.\n\nCheck: Flow totals and directions reconcile with the source. Abstract connection paths are distinguished from observed travel routes. Preserve genuine spatial intersections through separate views rather than false rerouting.\n\nPitfalls: Interpreting curved graphic connections as measured transport paths. Bundling unrelated flows in a way that invents shared geography.\n\nExample: A regional freight view separates outbound shipments by origin hub, with geographic destinations and a declared width scale for shipment volume.\n\nAlternatives: Reference map, Transit map."
